<Section id="each" title="2. Drawing an Entire Dataset">

We drew one song. With 139 of them, we don't want to copy that markup 139 times — we want to repeat it once per row. Svelte's **`{#each}` block** does exactly that: give it a list and it stamps out the HTML inside it once for every item.

We'll start small, with the first six songs. `data.slice(0, 6)` takes items 0 to 5:

```svelte
/// file: Loop over the first few
{#each data.slice(0, 6) as row, i}
	<circle r={row.popularity} />
{/each}
```

`row` is the song for this turn of the loop, and `i` is its **index** — its position in the list (0, 1, 2, …).

Each song needs its own spot in a grid. We wrap every cell in a `<g>` — a **group** that holds several shapes and can be moved as one — and shift it into place with a `transform`. The grid maths lives in a helper, `cellPosition(i)`, so the HTML stays about the drawing:

```svelte
/// file: One group per cell
{#each data.slice(0, 6) as row, i}
	<g transform={cellPosition(i)}>
		<rect ... />   <!-- the cell's border -->
		<circle ... /> <!-- sized by popularity -->
		<text>{row.title}</text>
	</g>
{/each}
```

Inside each group we draw two circles — a dark disc with a small light label, so each cell reads like a little **vinyl record** — plus the title and artist text and a `<rect>` marking the cell boundary. Swap `data.slice(0, 6)` for `data` and the loop draws **all 139 songs** at once.

---

One snag: popularity is measured from `0` to `100`, which is too big to use straight as a radius — a value of `83` would be a circle wider than its cell. We remap that `0–100` range onto a radius range that fits, with a **scale** from D3 — `scaleLinear`. We keep the low end at `0`, so the least popular songs shrink to nothing, which is honest to the data:

```svelte
/// file: A scale for the radius
import { scaleLinear } from 'd3';

const radius = scaleLinear()
	.domain([0, 100]) // popularity runs from 0 to 100
	.range([0, 95]); // 0 -> invisible, 100 -> biggest dot
```

A scale is just a mapping: every value in the **domain** lands on a matching value in the **range**, in proportion. Drag the handles below to feel how an input maps to an output — and edit the four numbers to reshape the mapping:

<ScaleDemo />

Now size each circle with `r={radius(row.popularity)}`. Fill in the cells in the exercise:

<Exercise file="5-drawing-an-entire-dataset.svelte" bleed>
	<DrawingEntireDataset />
	{#snippet result()}
		<FullGridDemo />
	{/snippet}
</Exercise>

{#snippet sidebar()}

**Repeat markup with `{#each}`**

```svelte
/// file: Each
{#each data as row, i}
	<!-- drawn once per song; `row` is the song, `i` its index -->
{/each}
```

> [!Note] New words
> - **`{#each}` block**: repeats the markup inside it once per item in a list.
> - **index** (`i`): an item's position in the list, counting from `0`.
> - **`<g>`**: a group holding several shapes so they move together.
> - **scale**: a function that maps values from one range onto another.

**Position each cell**

`cellPosition(i)` returns the grid offset for cell `i`:

```svelte
/// file: Create a tile for a song
<g transform={cellPosition(i)}>
	<rect x="0" y="0" width={CELL} height={CELL} fill="none" stroke="#ddd" />
	<!-- the record: a dark disc with a small light label -->
	<circle cx="100" cy="100" r={radius(row.popularity)} fill="#351D13" />
	<circle cx="100" cy="100" r={radius(row.popularity) * 0.3} fill="#fff" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="4" />
</g>
```

**Map popularity to a radius**

```svelte
/// file: Scale
const radius = scaleLinear()
	.domain([0, 100])
	.range([0, 95]);
```

**RESULT** (all 139 songs):

